The `+` lines contain no non-ASCII character, no backtick, no dollar sign and no backslash. The only double quotes are the pair around `"no restriction"` inside the comment.

## The test — CREATE THE NEW FILE `services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1233-in-redis-mode-platform-settings-expires.test.ts`

**Reference for the `vi.hoisted` shape (READ-ONLY, do not modify, do not add it to your diff):**
`services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1204-a-non-array-allow-list-fails-closed-and-documenttype-wins.test.ts:34` — `const catalogue = vi.hoisted(() => ({ types: [] as Array<Record<string, unknown>> }));` followed by a `vi.mock(...)` factory that reads it. **IGNORE the reference test named in the input** (`ks501-…`): it MOCKS `../services/redis`, which is the exact opposite of what this file must do — this file drives the REAL `redis.ts` over a FAKE `ioredis`.

`vi.hoisted` is required and is not optional: `vi.mock` factories are hoisted above the imports, so a plain top-level `const` would not exist yet when the factory runs.

Write EXACTLY this file. It is three cells: one 🔴 and two controls.

```
/**
 * =============================================================================
 * KS-1233 - in Redis mode platform-settings must not expire
 * =============================================================================
 * redis.ts:683 wrote every notif:<key> with setex(..., TTL.privacy = 86400, ...)
 * and the only writer of platform-settings is PUT /api/admin/settings
 * (routes/admin.ts:1127), so 24 h after the last admin write the key was gone,
 * verification.ts read the absence as integrations [] - no restriction - and
 * every restricted connector became unrestricted (#1035 gate finding N-A).
 *
 * These cells drive the REAL services/redis over a FAKE ioredis that records
 * every write and its expiry. No Redis server is started.
 * =============================================================================
 */
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, vi } from 'vitest';

const H = vi.hoisted(() => ({ writes: [] as Array<{ verb: string; key: string; ttl: number | null }> }));

vi.mock('ioredis', () => ({
  default: class FakeRedis {
    constructor(_url?: string, _opts?: unknown) { /* initRedis only calls the methods below */ }
    on(): this { return this; }
    async connect(): Promise<void> { /* the fake is always connected */ }
    async ping(): Promise<string> { return 'PONG'; }
    async get(): Promise<string | null> { return null; }
    async set(key: string, _value: string): Promise<string> { H.writes.push({ verb: 'set', key, ttl: null }); return 'OK'; }
    async setex(key: string, ttl: number, _value: string): Promise<string> { H.writes.push({ verb: 'setex', key, ttl }); return 'OK'; }
  },
}));

import { initRedis, isRedisAvailable, setNotificationSettings } from '../services/redis';

const PLATFORM_KEY = 'secuura:gateway:notif:platform-settings';
const OTHER_KEY = 'secuura:gateway:notif:notif-defaults';

beforeAll(async () => {
  await initRedis();
});

describe('KS-1233: platform-settings is configuration, not a cache entry', () => {
  it('control: these cells run in REDIS mode, not the in-memory fallback', () => {
    expect(isRedisAvailable()).toBe(true);
  });

  it('🔴 an admin write of platform-settings is stored with NO expiry', async () => {
    H.writes.length = 0;
    await setNotificationSettings('platform-settings', { integrations: [{ connectorId: 'ks1233-c1', allowedDocumentTypes: ['SSD_DOCUMENT'] }] });
    expect(H.writes).toEqual([{ verb: 'set', key: PLATFORM_KEY, ttl: null }]);
  });

  it('control: every OTHER notification key keeps the 24 h TTL it has today', async () => {
    H.writes.length = 0;
    await setNotificationSettings('notif-defaults', { email: true });
    expect(H.writes).toEqual([{ verb: 'setex', key: OTHER_KEY, ttl: 86400 }]);
  });
});
```

## THE RED-FIRST CELL — state it to yourself before you write a line

**At the UNTOUCHED tip, `🔴 an admin write of platform-settings is stored with NO expiry` MUST FAIL, by assertion.** At the tip `setNotificationSettings` reaches `:683` and calls `setex`, so the fake records `[{ verb: 'setex', key: 'secuura:gateway:notif:platform-settings', ttl: 86400 }]` and `.toEqual([{ verb: 'set', key: PLATFORM_KEY, ttl: null }])` fails on both the verb and the ttl. **That failure is the proof of the defect.** After your product hunk the same call takes the new `key === 'platform-settings'` branch, records `set` with no ttl, and the cell is GREEN.

The two controls are GREEN on BOTH trees: Redis mode is entered either way, and `notif-defaults` still takes the `else` branch and still records `setex … 86400`. The second control is also what proves your change stayed NARROW — if you delete the TTL for every key it goes RED and the run fails.

## Red cells

- 🔴 an admin write of platform-settings is stored with NO expiry

## Output

Exactly ONE ```diff block, nothing outside it, containing TWO sections in this order:
1. `--- a/services/api-gateway/src/services/redis.ts` / `+++ b/services/api-gateway/src/services/redis.ts`, ONE hunk `@@ -682,4 +682,6 @@` — context 682, **1** `-` line (683), **3** `+` lines, then context 684/685. None of them empty.
2. `--- /dev/null` / `+++ b/services/api-gateway/src/__tests__/ks1233-in-redis-mode-platform-settings-expires.test.ts`, ONE hunk whose header's new-count equals the number of lines you actually emit (count them; do not guess), every line a `+`.

